From: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mt7993: keep the PCIe MAC out of L0s/L1.x during firmware download

The WM ROM patch download dies at the very last step on AN7581:

  mt7993 0000:01:00.0: mt7993_check_fwdl_state(): check target stat=1, cur state=1
  mt7993 0000:01:00.0: HW/SW Version: 0x8a108a10, Build Time: 20250623123248
  pcieport 0000:00:00.0: AER: Correctable error, Physical Layer (RxErr)
  pcieport 0000:00:00.0: AER: Uncorrectable (Fatal), Data Link Layer (SDES)
  mt7993 0000:01:00.0: Failed to start patch

i.e. the endpoint leaves the bus about 20 ms after MCU_CMD_PATCH_FINISH_REQ.
The ROM itself is healthy: the state readback and the 12 KB scatter download
both succeed, and the failure reproduces identically at Gen1 and Gen2, so it
is not signal integrity on the link.

MT7993 uses the same MediaTek PCIe MAC IP as MT7921, and MediaTek documented
this exact failure mode for it ("wifi: mt76: mt7921e: fix random fw download
fail", Deren Wu, 2022): once the MAC enters L0s while the patch payload is
being pulled in, the payload is corrupted, the CPU jumps into garbage and the
device falls off the bus.  The cure there was to force the MAC's own low power
state machine off, not to touch LNKCTL.

PCIE_LOW_POWER_CTRL_0 (chip 0x74030194, BAR0 + 0x10194) reports
force_dis_lowpwr[11:8] = 0 on this board, so L0s is allowed.  Set all four
bits before the first MCU transaction, which is exactly where the vendor's own
mt7993_pci_l1ss() puts them.

Signed-off-by: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
---
--- a/wlan_hwifi/chips/mt7993/mt7993.c
+++ b/wlan_hwifi/chips/mt7993/mt7993.c
@@ -954,6 +954,14 @@ static void mt7993_dbg_exit(struct pci_trans *ptrans)
 #endif
 }
 
+/*
+ * PCIE_LOW_POWER_CTRL_0 lives in the PCIe MAC block: chip address
+ * 0x74030194, i.e. BAR0 + 0x10194 (see the 0x1_xxxx -> 0x7403_xxxx
+ * window documented in mt_wifi/chip/mt7993/mt7993.c).
+ */
+#define MT7993_PCIE_LOW_POWER_CTRL_0	0x10194
+#define MT7993_FORCE_DIS_LOWPWR		0x00000f00
+
 static int
 mt7993_driver_own(struct mtk_hw_dev *dev, struct mtk_bus_trans *trans, bool drv_own)
 {
@@ -962,6 +970,29 @@ mt7993_driver_own(struct mtk_hw_dev *dev, struct mtk_bus_trans *trans, bool drv_
 	u32 target_state = (drv_own ? 0 : 1)
 			<< CONN_HOST_CSR_TOP_WF_BAND0_LPCTL_WF_B0_AP_HOST_OWNER_STATE_SYNC_SHFT;
 
+	/*
+	 * MT7993 shares the MediaTek PCIe MAC IP with MT7921, and MediaTek
+	 * documented this exact failure for that block: the ROM patch payload
+	 * is corrupted once the MAC enters L0s while it is being downloaded,
+	 * the WM CPU then jumps into garbage and the endpoint falls off the
+	 * bus.  Observed here as AER RxErr + SDES a few milliseconds after
+	 * MCU_CMD_PATCH_FINISH_REQ, followed by "Failed to start patch".
+	 *
+	 * force_dis_lowpwr[11:8] = 0xf keeps L0s, L1, L1.1 and L1.2 disabled
+	 * for the download and for the MCU exchange that follows it.
+	 * The vendor does the same from mt7993_pci_l1ss().
+	 */
+	if (drv_own) {
+		u32 lp;
+
+		lp = bus_read(trans, MT7993_PCIE_LOW_POWER_CTRL_0);
+		bus_write(trans, MT7993_PCIE_LOW_POWER_CTRL_0,
+			  lp | MT7993_FORCE_DIS_LOWPWR);
+		dev_info(trans->pdev,
+			 "pcie low power ctrl 0x%08x -> 0x%08x\n", lp,
+			 bus_read(trans, MT7993_PCIE_LOW_POWER_CTRL_0));
+	}
+
 	if (drv_own)
 		bus_write(trans, reg,
 		CONN_HOST_CSR_TOP_WF_BAND0_LPCTL_WF_B0_AP_HOST_CLR_FW_OWN_HS_PULSE_MASK);
